    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11000v1</id>
    <title>Efficient Plane-to-Point Energy Transfer from Supramolecular Excitonic Systems to Molecular Acceptors</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T22:49:59Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11000v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11000v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Collective excitonic interactions in supramolecular assemblies offer opportunities for efficient light harvesting and directed energy transport. Here, we demonstrate efficient energy transfer from two-dimensional silica-encapsulated TDBC J-aggregate sheets to covalently attached ATTO 590 molecular acceptors. Silica encapsulation preserves the J-aggregate excitonic properties while enabling acceptor functionalization at a defined separation from the donor plane. Steady-state donor quenching and acceptor sensitization yield energy-transfer efficiencies of 75-81% in solution, while time-resolved photoluminescence measurements reveal donor lifetime shortening. We develop a generalized Forster resonance energy-transfer model for an extended planar donor coupled to localized molecular acceptors, predicting an inverse fourth-power dependence of the transfer rate on donor-acceptor separation. Using experimental spectroscopic data and estimated structural parameters, the model predicts an energy-transfer rate of 4.77 x 10^10 s^-1, approximately ten times faster than the intrinsic donor decay rate, corresponding to a theoretical energy-transfer efficiency of approximately 91%. These findings provide a framework for developing hybrid excitonic architectures for light harvesting.</summary>
